## Bug

When the Paperclip server shuts down or crashes, child agent processes spawned via `runChildProcess` are **not killed**, causing them to accumulate as orphaned processes that consume memory until manually cleaned up.

### Root Cause

`runChildProcess` in `packages/adapter-utils/src/server-utils.ts` spawns every agent subprocess with `detached: true` (line ~1946), making each process the leader of its own process group. This is correct for targeted kill semantics, but it means systemd's `KillMode=control-group` cannot reach these processes when it kills the service cgroup.

Additionally, `shutdownAppServices()` in `server/src/app.ts` never iterates `runningProcesses` to kill them:

```typescript
const shutdownAppServices = () => {
  // ...closes watchers, disposes services...
  // ❌ never kills entries in runningProcesses
};
```

### Impact

Each server restart leaves all in-flight agent runs as permanent orphans. During a crash loop (e.g. the embedded postgres port conflict in issue #7047), this compounds rapidly:
- 18+ restarts × N agents per restart = 100+ orphaned `claude --tools` processes
- On this machine: **113 processes consuming 15.8 GB RAM**, making Windows unusable
- Agent heartbeats appear healthy (processes are running) but cannot report back to Paperclip (their run IDs are dead), so routine execution issues stay stuck `in_progress` blocking all future dispatches

### Fix

Export `signalRunningProcess` from `server-utils.ts` and call it for every entry in `runningProcesses` inside `shutdownAppServices()`:

```typescript
const shutdownAppServices = () => {
  // ...existing cleanup...
  for (const running of runningProcesses.values()) {
    try { signalRunningProcess(running, "SIGTERM"); } catch { /* already gone */ }
  }
  runningProcesses.clear();
};
```

### Files affected

- `packages/adapter-utils/src/server-utils.ts` — export `signalRunningProcess`
- `server/src/adapters/utils.ts` — re-export `signalRunningProcess`
- `server/src/app.ts` — kill `runningProcesses` in `shutdownAppServices`

### Related

- Issue #7047 (embedded postgres port conflict) — crash loop that triggered this bug

## Bug

`hermes_local` adapter's stdout-regex cost/token capture (`TOKEN_USAGE_REGEX`, `COST_REGEX` in `packages/adapters/hermes-local/src/shared/constants.ts`) **fails silently** for Hermes Agent's current quiet-mode output, leaving `agent.spentMonthlyCents` stuck at `0` regardless of actual usage.

```ts
// Current
export const TOKEN_USAGE_REGEX =
  /tokens?[:\s]+(\d+)\s*(?:input|in)\b.*?(\d+)\s*(?:output|out)\b/i;
export const COST_REGEX = /(?:cost|spent)[:\s]*\$?([\d.]+)/i;
```

These regexes assume Hermes emits a footer line like `tokens: 1500 input, 200 output` and `cost: $0.05` on stdout. **Hermes v0.13.0 (`-Q` quiet mode) emits neither.**

Reproducible with:

```bash
$ hermes chat -q "say only OK" -Q 2>/tmp/err 1>/tmp/out
$ cat /tmp/out
OK
$ cat /tmp/err
session_id: 20260526_060822_8473b8
$ grep -iE 'token|cost|spent' /tmp/out /tmp/err
# (no matches)
```

So `parseHermesOutput()` populates `result.sessionId` from `SESSION_ID_REGEX` (works), but never fills `result.usage` or `result.costUsd`. Cost stays `0` for every run, every model.

## Why this is the same root cause as #2922

[#2922](https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/issues/2922) shows the regex matching **prose in agent output** (false positives → inflated cost). This issue is the symmetric failure: when prose doesn't contain a "cost: $..." substring at all, capture is zero. **Both modes fail because stdout regex on free-form English isn't a reliable contract.** Tightening the regex (#2922's fix) helps with false positives but doesn't help my case — Hermes simply doesn't print these lines.

## Suggested fix: read from `~/.hermes/state.db`

Hermes already records exact usage in its `sessions` SQLite table:

```sql
-- ~/.hermes/state.db
CREATE TABLE sessions (
  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  model TEXT,
  input_tokens INTEGER,
  output_tokens INTEGER,
  cache_read_tokens INTEGER,
  cache_write_tokens INTEGER,
  reasoning_tokens INTEGER,
  estimated_cost_usd REAL,
  actual_cost_usd REAL,
  cost_status TEXT,
  cost_source TEXT,
  -- ...
);
```

The adapter already extracts `sessionId` from stdout via `SESSION_ID_REGEX`. After the run completes, it can deterministically read that exact session's row from `state.db`, instead of regex-scraping prose. Sketch in `packages/adapters/hermes-local/src/server/execute.ts`:

```ts
import Database from "better-sqlite3";
import path from "node:path";
import os from "node:os";

interface HermesSessionUsage {
  inputTokens: number;
  outputTokens: number;
  cacheReadTokens?: number;
  cacheWriteTokens?: number;
  costUsd?: number;
  model?: string;
}

function readSessionUsage(sessionId: string): HermesSessionUsage | null {
  const dbPath = process.env.HERMES_STATE_DB
    || path.join(os.homedir(), ".hermes", "state.db");
  try {
    const db = new Database(dbPath, { readonly: true, fileMustExist: true });
    const row = db.prepare(
      `SELECT model, input_tokens, output_tokens,
              cache_read_tokens, cache_write_tokens,
              estimated_cost_usd, actual_cost_usd
         FROM sessions WHERE id = ?`,
    ).get(sessionId) as any;
    db.close();
    if (!row) return null;
    return {
      model: row.model ?? undefined,
      inputTokens: row.input_tokens ?? 0,
      outputTokens: row.output_tokens ?? 0,
      cacheReadTokens: row.cache_read_tokens ?? 0,
      cacheWriteTokens: row.cache_write_tokens ?? 0,
      costUsd: (row.actual_cost_usd ?? row.estimated_cost_usd) || undefined,
    };
  } catch {
    return null;  // fall back to regex; never block a run on usage failure
  }
}
```

Then in `parseHermesOutput()`, after `sessionId` is captured:

```ts
if (result.sessionId) {
  const dbUsage = readSessionUsage(result.sessionId);
  if (dbUsage) {
    result.usage = {
      inputTokens: dbUsage.inputTokens,
      outputTokens: dbUsage.outputTokens,
    };
    if (dbUsage.costUsd !== undefined) {
      result.costUsd = dbUsage.costUsd;
    }
  }
}
// Existing regex remains as a fallback for adapters/versions that DO

## Summary

`payload.prompt` in `request_confirmation` issue-thread interactions is rendered as a raw text node instead of through the markdown renderer. Markdown (`**bold**`, `` `code` ``, `[link](url)`, lists) appears as plain text in the UI, breaking confirmation workflows where the proposer relies on the same markdown conventions used in plan documents and comments.

This is regression-prone because the sibling field `payload.detailsMarkdown` in the same component **does** route through `MarkdownBody`, so the bug is a one-line omission inside the pending-state branch of `RequestConfirmationCard`.

## Reproduction

1. From any agent or user with permission, create a pending `request_confirmation` interaction whose `payload.prompt` contains markdown, e.g.:

   ```json
   POST /api/issues/{issueId}/interactions
   {
     "kind": "request_confirmation",
     "payload": {
       "version": 1,
       "prompt": "**Bold** and `code` and [a link](https://example.com).",
       "acceptLabel": "Yes",
       "rejectLabel": "No"
     }
   }
   ```
2. Open the issue in the web UI and observe the pending confirmation card.

### Expected

The prompt renders with `<strong>Bold</strong>`, `<code>code</code>`, and an anchor for the link — the same way `payload.detailsMarkdown` already renders.

### Actual

The prompt renders the literal characters `**Bold** and \`code\` and [a link](https://example.com).` as plain text.

## Root cause

`ui/src/components/IssueThreadInteractionCard.tsx`, inside `RequestConfirmationCard`, around line 1097–1099:

```tsx
<div className="text-sm leading-6 text-foreground">
  {interaction.payload.prompt}
</div>
```

vs the sibling block 2 lines below (working correctly):

```tsx
{interaction.payload.detailsMarkdown ? (
  <div className="border-t border-border/60 pt-3 text-sm">
    <MarkdownBody>{interaction.payload.detailsMarkdown}</MarkdownBody>
  </div>
) : null}
```

Wrapping `interaction.payload.prompt` in `<MarkdownBody>` brings the two fields back into consistency and matches the markdown policy used everywhere else (issue descriptions, comments, plan documents).

## Suggested patch

```diff
diff --git a/ui/src/components/IssueThreadInteractionCard.tsx b/ui/src/components/IssueThreadInteractionCard.tsx
@@ -1095,7 +1095,7 @@ function RequestConfirmationCard({
       {interaction.status === "pending" ? (
         <div className="space-y-3 rounded-sm border border-border/70 bg-background/75 p-4">
           <div className="text-sm leading-6 text-foreground">
-            {interaction.payload.prompt}
+            <MarkdownBody>{interaction.payload.prompt}</MarkdownBody>
           </div>
```

The same component already imports `MarkdownBody` from `./MarkdownBody` and uses it for `detailsMarkdown`, so the change is one line plus a corresponding test.

A complementary test in `ui/src/components/IssueThreadInteractionCard.test.tsx` (full patch attached on AI-6 internal tracker):

```tsx
it("renders markdown in request confirmation prompts", () => {
  const host = renderCard({
    interaction: {
      ...pendingRequestConfirmationInteraction,
      payload: {
        ...pendingRequestConfirmationInteraction.payload,
        prompt: "**Bold** and `code` and [a link](https://example.com).",
      },
    },
  });

  const prompt = host.querySelector(".paperclip-markdown");
  expect(prompt).toBeTruthy();
  expect(prompt?.querySelector("strong")?.textContent).toBe("Bold");
  expect(prompt?.querySelector("code")?.textContent).toBe("code");
  const link = prompt?.querySelector("a");
  expect(link?.getAttribute("href")).toBe("https://example.com");
  expect(link?.textContent).toBe("a link");
});
```

I have verified locally:

- `pnpm --filter @paperclipai/ui exec vitest run src/components/IssueThreadInteractionCard.test.tsx` — 12/12 passing (11 existing + 1 new).
- Visually confirmed in a `local_trusted` instance against the bundled UI (hot-patched the published `index-*.js` for an in-place verification on `paperclipai/serv

## Title

isIssuePrefixConflict doesn't unwrap DrizzleQueryError.cause — creating a company after archiving one returns 500

## Body

When a company is archived and you try to create a new one (even with a different name), `POST /api/companies` returns a **500 Internal Server Error**.

### Root Cause

`isIssuePrefixConflict()` in `server/src/services/companies.ts` checks `error.code` and `error.constraint` directly on the caught error object. However, Drizzle ORM wraps the underlying Postgres error inside a `DrizzleQueryError`, storing the actual Postgres error (with `code: "23505"` and `constraint: "companies_issue_prefix_idx"`) in **`error.cause`** — not on the top-level error.

Since `DrizzleQueryError` doesn't have `.code` or `.constraint` at the top level, `isIssuePrefixConflict()` returns `false`, the error is re-thrown, and the user gets a 500 instead of a graceful retry with a different prefix.

### Affected Code

`server/src/services/companies.ts` — `isIssuePrefixConflict()` function (npm v2026.517.0):

```typescript
function isIssuePrefixConflict(error: unknown) {
    const constraint = typeof error === "object" && error !== null && "constraint" in error
        ? error.constraint
        : typeof error === "object" && error !== null && "constraint_name" in error
            ? error.constraint_name
            : undefined;
    return typeof error === "object"
        && error !== null
        && "code" in error
        && error.code === "23505"
        && constraint === "companies_issue_prefix_idx";
}
```

### Fixed Version (already on `master`)

The `master` branch already has a fix that traverses `error.cause`:

```typescript
function isIssuePrefixConflict(error: unknown) {
    const seen = new Set<unknown>();
    let current = error;
    while (typeof current === "object" && current !== null && !seen.has(current)) {
      seen.add(current);
      const maybe = current as { code?: string; constraint?: string; constraint_name?: string; cause?: unknown };
      const constraint = maybe.constraint ?? maybe.constraint_name;
      if (maybe.code === "23505" && constraint === "companies_issue_prefix_idx") {
        return true;
      }
      current = maybe.cause;
    }
    return false;
}
```

### Impact

- Users cannot create a new company after archiving one (issue prefix from the archived company still occupies the unique index)
- Any unexpected unique constraint violation on `companies_issue_prefix_idx` surfaces as a 500 instead of a retry

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Create a company named "Neighbor-OS" (gets issue prefix `NEI`)
2. Archive that company
3. Try to create any new company that derives the same base prefix (e.g., "NeighborOS-V2" → `NEO`)
4. API returns 500

### Workaround

Patch `isIssuePrefixConflict` in the installed npm package to traverse `error.cause`. The fix is already on `master` — this issue tracks getting it into a release.

### Environment

- npm package version: 2026.517.0
- @paperclipai/server: (same version)
- Drizzle ORM wraps Postgres errors in `DrizzleQueryError` with original error at `.cause`

## Goal

Fix the Paperclip-server PATCH semantics so that `PATCH /api/agents/:id` with `adapterConfig.env = { NEW_KEY: ... }` preserves all unsubmitted env keys, allows explicit deletion (`null`), and only applies strict-secret-mode validation to keys actually submitted in the PATCH body.

This bug caused the Agent Runtime Architect's `PAPERCLIP_API_KEY` to be wiped when an unrelated PATCH set new `HONCHO_*` keys, and currently makes any "read+merge+PATCH" workaround impossible under strict-secret-mode whenever a legacy plain sensitive key is already persisted.

Tracking context: Eli Board ELI-213 (recovery chain ELI-211 → ELI-195 workstream 1).

## Bug sites

### 1. Shallow merge clobbers nested `env`

`server/src/routes/agents.ts:2628-2631`:

```ts
let rawEffectiveAdapterConfig = requestedAdapterConfig ?? existingAdapterConfig;
if (requestedAdapterConfig && !changingAdapterType && !replaceAdapterConfig) {
  rawEffectiveAdapterConfig = { ...existingAdapterConfig, ...requestedAdapterConfig };
}
```

`env` is a top-level key of `adapterConfig`, so any PATCH that supplies `env` replaces the *entire* env map. Existing keys (including legacy plain sensitive ones like `PAPERCLIP_API_KEY`) are dropped.

### 2. Strict-secret-mode validates every key, not just submitted keys

`server/src/services/secrets.ts:660-668`:

```ts
const binding = canonicalizeBinding(parsed.data as EnvBinding);
if (binding.type === "plain") {
  if (opts?.strictMode && isSensitiveEnvKey(key) && binding.value.trim().length > 0) {
    throw unprocessable(
      `Strict secret mode requires secret references for sensitive key: ${key}`,
    );
  }
  ...
}
```

Once we deep-merge in (1), strict mode would still 422 on a legacy plain sensitive key the caller never touched in this PATCH — so deep-merge alone is not enough; we must scope strict-mode enforcement to *submitted* keys.

## Required behavior

A `PATCH` with `body.adapterConfig.env = { NEW_KEY: ... }` must:

1. **Preserve all unsubmitted env keys** (deep-merge by key). Bindings already persisted (including `secret_ref` and legacy `plain`) pass through untouched.
2. **Allow explicit deletion**: `env.K = null` removes key `K` from the persisted env map.
3. **Strict-secret-mode applies only to submitted keys.** Plain sensitive bindings that are *not* in the current PATCH body must not 422.
4. **`replaceAdapterConfig=true` (PUT-style) preserves current behavior**: env is replaced wholesale; no deep-merge.
5. **Adapter-type change path** (`changingAdapterType=true`): env is already preserved via `ADAPTER_AGNOSTIC_KEYS` (`agents.ts:2632-2649`). If the new PATCH body *also* supplies `env`, deep-merge applies as in (1) so a type change combined with a partial env edit doesn't silently clobber. (Out of scope to redesign type-change semantics; just ensure env merging is consistent.)

## Implementation plan

### A. Thread submitted env keys through to the secrets normalizer

`server/src/services/secrets.ts` — extend `NormalizeEnvOptions`:

```ts
export interface NormalizeEnvOptions {
  strictMode?: boolean;
  fieldPath?: string;
  /**
   * When set, strict-secret-mode is enforced ONLY for keys in this set.
   * Used by PATCH paths so that legacy plain sensitive bindings that were
   * already persisted (and not touched by this PATCH) do not retroactively
   * fail strict-mode validation. Undefined/absent = enforce on all keys
   * (existing behaviour, used by POST/replace paths).
   */
  strictModeKeys?: ReadonlySet<string>;
}
```

Update the check at `secrets.ts:663-668`:

```ts
if (binding.type === "plain") {
  const strictApplies =
    opts?.strictMode &&
    (opts.strictModeKeys === undefined || opts.strictModeKeys.has(key)) &&
    isSensitiveEnvKey(key) &&
    binding.value.trim().length > 0;
  if (strictApplies) {
    throw unprocessable(
      `Strict secret mode requires secret references for sensitive key: ${key}`,
    );
  }
  ...
}
```

Forward `strictModeKeys` through `normalizeAdapterConfigForPersistenceI

## Package / version
`@paperclipai/server 2026.428.0`
- `routes/issues.js:76-85` — `shouldImplicitlyMoveCommentedIssueToTodo`
- `services/heartbeat.js:4942-4954` — deferred-wake promotion path

## Observed behavior
A Board/user-set `done` status is not terminal. Two code paths independently reverse it without checking who set the status:

**Path 1 — `routes/issues.js:76-85` (`shouldImplicitlyMoveCommentedIssueToTodo`):**
Any comment on a `done`/`cancelled`/`blocked` issue with an assignee triggers an implicit `todo` promotion, regardless of whether the status was set by a board actor or an agent.

**Path 2 — `services/heartbeat.js:4942-4954` (deferred-wake):**
A deferred human/comment wake on a `done`/`cancelled` issue promotes status to `todo` (`source=deferred_comment_wake`, `reopenedFrom=done`), again without an actor-role check.

**Reproduction evidence (2026-05-09, local deployment):**

| Issue | Event |
|---|---|
| Board-set `done` issue | Platform reverted to `todo` within seconds (×2 in same day) |
| Board audit comment | Triggered `in_progress` reopen via same mechanism |

## Expected behavior
If the current status was set by a board/user actor (`statusSetByUserId` or equivalent field on the status row), the implicit-reopen logic must be suppressed:
- **Comment path:** record the comment as-is; do not change status.
- **Deferred-wake path:** run the heartbeat in notify-only mode; do not modify status.

Normal agent-to-agent comment-reopen behavior should be unaffected.

## Blast radius
Low. The guard is additive — it only fires when `statusSetByUserId` points to a board/user actor.

## Suggested fix

**Patch 1 — `routes/issues.js:76-85`:**
```js
// before:
if (issue.status === 'done' || issue.status === 'cancelled') {
  return { shouldMove: true, targetStatus: 'todo' }
}

// after:
if (issue.status === 'done' || issue.status === 'cancelled') {
  if (issue.statusSetByUserId) return { shouldMove: false }  // board/user-set terminal
  return { shouldMove: true, targetStatus: 'todo' }
}
```

**Patch 2 — `services/heartbeat.js:4942-4954`:**
```js
// before:
if (issue.status === 'done' || issue.status === 'cancelled') {
  await updateIssueStatus(issue.id, 'todo', { source: 'deferred_comment_wake' })
}

// after:
if (issue.status === 'done' || issue.status === 'cancelled') {
  if (issue.statusSetByUserId) {
    // notify-only: do not touch status
  } else {
    await updateIssueStatus(issue.id, 'todo', { source: 'deferred_comment_wake' })
  }
}
```

Related: #6598, #6599.

## Summary

The heartbeat's "skill mentions referenced from this issue" lookup assumes every value extracted from a `skill://...` markdown link is a UUID and feeds it directly into `inArray(companySkillsTable.id, …)`. When an author (human or agent) writes the shorter form `[research-synthesis](skill://research-synthesis)` instead of the canonical `[research-synthesis](skill://<uuid>?s=research-synthesis)`, the slug ends up in the `inArray` and Postgres aborts the query with `operator does not exist: uuid = text`, which surfaces as `adapter_failed` and the heartbeat dies before the agent can run.

In practice this fires whenever an agent (e.g. a Claude-backed assistant) writes a status comment that naturally references a skill — the short slug form reads better in markdown source than a UUID, so agents reach for it.

## Reproduction

Paperclip 2026.517.0, authenticated mode, default local instance.

1. In any issue comment, paste a markdown link like:
   ```markdown
   [research-synthesis](skill://research-synthesis)
   ```
   (i.e. omit the `?s=` query param and put the slug in the hostname slot).
2. Run the assigned agent's heartbeat (any work mode).
3. Heartbeat aborts with `adapter_failed`. Server log:
   ```
   Failed query: select "id", "key" from "company_skills"
     where ("company_skills"."company_id" = $1
            and "company_skills"."id" in ($2))
     params: <company-uuid>, research-synthesis
   ```

The real-world comment that surfaced this in our install:

```markdown
... the [`research-synthesis`](skill://research-synthesis) discipline exists to catch
*before* an artefact moves toward publication ...
```

Four agents heartbeating against the same issue simultaneously all crashed in a ~400ms window; nothing retried; the issue stopped receiving heartbeats. The system "recovered" only by avoiding that issue.

